+# Feature Specification: JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing
+### User Story 1 - Apply a patch (P1)
+A service author calls `applyPatch(document, patch)` with a JSON document and an array of operations and gets the patched document back as a new value. The input document and patch are never mutated, and the result shares no structure with the patch.
+Acceptance:
+1. `add` sets an object member (replacing an existing one) or inserts into an array at an index `0..length`; `-` appends; path `""` replaces the whole document.
+2. `remove` deletes an existing member or array element (later elements shift down).
+3. `replace` sets the value of an existing member or element.
+4. `move` removes the value at `from` and adds it at `path`; `copy` adds a deep copy of the value at `from`.
+5. `test` passes when the value at `path` equals `value` (JSON equality: object member order ignored, array order significant, numbers by value, types strict); otherwise it throws.
+6. Operations apply in order, each on the result of the previous one.
+
+### User Story 2 - Fail loudly (P1)
+1. A patch that is not an array, an operation that is not an object, an unknown/missing `op`, a missing or non-string `path`/`from`, or a missing `value` (add, replace, test) throws.
+2. A failing operation (missing target, out-of-range index, failed `test`, invalid pointer) throws, and no partial result is returned.
+
+### Edge cases
+- `value: null` is a valid value; only an absent `value` member is invalid.
+- `move` whose `from` is a proper prefix of `path` throws; `from` equal to `path` is a no-op (after checking `from` exists).
+- `remove` of the root (`""`) throws (no document would remain).
+- `-` and indexes with leading zeros or signs are invalid for remove, replace, test and `from`; add accepts `-` only.
+- Members named `__proto__` are ordinary data and never touch prototypes.
+
+## Requirements
+- FR-001 `applyPatch(document, patch)` is exported from `src/index.ts`.
+- FR-002 Semantics follow RFC 6902 sections 4 and 5, with pointers per RFC 6901.
+- FR-003 Inputs are not mutated; the result is a new value.
+- FR-004 Invalid patches and failed operations throw an `Error`.
+
+## Success Criteria
+- The RFC 6902 appendix A examples (A.1–A.16) produce the specified results or errors.
+
+## Assumptions
+- Documents are plain parsed JSON. No dependencies are added.
